Slide 1: RCA Agent System Technical Deep Dive(Target: 25 seconds)
"Today, I’ll walk through how our RCA system turns raw pipeline alerts into automated investigations and safe, controlled fixes. Instead of just showing what we built, I will focus on the architectural patterns you can reuse in your own projects. In our environment, we have the advantage of strict, pre-reviewed SOPs. Towards the end, I’ll also share how you can adapt this design if your team doesn't have detailed playbooks yet."
Slide 2: Deterministic State Machine(Target: 1 minute 10 seconds)
"Our first major design decision is that standard application code—not the AI—owns the incident lifecycle. A deterministic orchestrator moves every ticket from NEW, through analysis and verification, to CLOSED. The AI models only contribute assessments inside those steps; an agent can never advance its own status or grant itself permission to run a fix.
We also strictly separate proposing, verifying, and executing. The Proposer generates an initial hypothesis from past tickets. The Verifier runs live diagnostic checks. And if a fix is needed, a separate, non-AI Executor runs an engineer-approved command.
Finally, we protect the upstream alerting database. Instead of writing closures directly to production tables, our backend pushes updates to an outbound queue for an external sync job to process. What you see here is the standard flow, though if new error logs arrive mid-investigation, the system automatically loops the incident back for a fresh analysis."
Slide 3: Idempotent Ingestion and Late Evidence(Target: 55 seconds)
"Before an AI can reason effectively, it needs clean, stable data. Our Batch Importer groups duplicate alerts by matching the DAG, category, exception type, and exact error message. It tracks the exact source row IDs we've already pulled, so polling the database repeatedly never creates duplicate work.
A tricky edge case in production is late-arriving evidence—when new error rows show up while an investigation is already running. When our importer detects new row IDs, it archives the old AI outputs, updates the error payload, and resets the ticket to NEW so the agents re-evaluate the full picture. That prevents an old conclusion from silently being applied to new errors.
Your grouping rules might differ—if your error messages contain dynamic IDs, you'll want to strip those out so incidents don't fragment. The core principle to carry over is maintaining a strict incident identity, tracking evidence versions, and automatically invalidating stale AI conclusions."
Slide 4: Onboarding Agent and Reviewed SOPs(Target: 1 minute 15 seconds)
"The Onboarding phase defines what the Verifier agent is allowed to investigate. We feed DAG code into our Onboarding Assistant, which extracts the diagnostic commands, connection IDs, and alert conditions to draft an SOP. An administrator then reviews that draft and maps the connections to our registered, read-only targets.
The challenge is that the code that triggers an alert isn't always the code you need to investigate it. In our FlipChip pipeline, for example, the DAG uses SELECT queries just to check filtering timestamps, and writes the actual error records using an INSERT statement. During onboarding, our system spots that INSERT table and proposes a read-only SELECT query against it so the Verifier can actually inspect the logged errors. The admin simply reviews the time window and row limit.
If a pipeline relies on external scripts that the agent can't access, admins can exclude them and log a coverage warning. The Verifier will still run the available checks, but because coverage is incomplete, it automatically caps its overall assessment at INCONCLUSIVE. While our setup relies heavily on reviewed SOPs, I'll cover on the final slide how teams without strict playbooks can handle this."
Slide 5: The Verifier Diagnostic ReAct Loop(Target: 1 minute 25 seconds)
"Once an incident reaches the Verifier, it investigates using a bounded Reason-and-Act—or ReAct—loop. It reads the error evidence, requests an authorized diagnostic from the SOP, reads the live output, and decides which SOP branch to follow next. We cap this budget at six tool calls so the agent always has a clear stopping point.
This is the truly agentic part of the platform: the model dynamically interprets live server outputs and adapts its next step, while our surrounding workflow strictly controls the incident lifecycle and execution permissions.
Critically, we hide the Proposer’s earlier hypothesis from the Verifier. We want the Verifier to inspect the live system with fresh eyes rather than anchoring on a historical guess. Every claim in its final report must cite the exact tool step that proved it, making every conclusion traceable.
While we constrain our Verifier to exact SOP commands, another team could instead expose typed diagnostic APIs—such as a tool that fetches logs for a specific incident window or reads equipment status. That allows the agent to investigate without needing a pre-written procedure for every scenario, provided you still enforce clear tool contracts and flag insufficient evidence."
Slide 6: Handling Transient Model Failures(Target: 55 seconds)
"When running LLMs in production, you have to separate AI infrastructure failures from actual system problems. If an LLM call times out, that just means the model gateway failed—it tells us nothing about whether the monitored pipeline is healthy.
When a timeout happens, our orchestrator releases the worker lease and schedules a retry using exponential backoff. We also save checkpoints of completed diagnostic steps so a brief network blip doesn't force the agent to re-run every server check from scratch.
However, resuming a checkpoint is only safe if the context hasn't changed. Before resuming, we verify that the hashes for both the error evidence and the SOP still match. If new error rows arrived or the SOP changed, we discard the checkpoint and start fresh. In your own system, you should also set a maximum age on saved observations, because a live server check from twenty minutes ago may no longer be current."
Slide 7: Execution Spec and Remediation Prechecks(Target: 1 minute 10 seconds)
"Remediation runs on a completely separate, non-AI path. The model can help explain an issue, but our deterministic Executor only runs versioned action specifications from an approved catalog. That specification strictly defines the command and the health checks around it.
After an engineer approves an action, the Executor claims a lease token and runs a live precheck first. If the target server has already recovered on its own, the Executor skips the command and marks the request ALREADY_HEALTHY.
If the target is still down, we run an Evidence Guard immediately before firing the command: we re-verify that the alert evidence in the database still matches what the engineer approved. This prevents the system from acting on an outdated incident. If it matches, the Executor runs the exact command and uses postchecks to verify recovery.
If you are building your own RCA system, your first version can easily stop at generating a reviewed diagnostic report. You can add automated remediation later once you have reliable action definitions and verification checks."
Slide 8: Security Boundaries and Guardrails
(Target: ~55 seconds)
"First, let's look at how we handle security. Any log or error message entering the AI is treated strictly as untrusted data. Even if an error log contains text telling the AI to run a command, the system won't do it—because our backend validates every tool and target independently of the AI's response.
On the diagnostic side, we enforce read-only database profiles and block any state-changing SQL or Linux commands. We also pin server keys and encrypt credentials so the agent can only connect to verified hosts.
If your team is building something similar, I recommend exposing narrow, structured tools whenever possible—for example, a tool that only accepts an Equipment ID and a time range, rather than letting the model pass raw shell text and relying on regex to catch bad patterns. The core rule is simple: the backend tool service, not the AI, must enforce what is allowed."
Slide 9: FlipChip Incident Walkthrough
(Target: ~1 minute 5 seconds)
"This FlipChip example shows why diagnostic scope matters. Here, the approved SOP runs a query to pull recent FlipChip error records—giving the Verifier the source, file name, error message, and timestamp so it can explain the situation to an engineer.
If rows come back, the agent matches them against the incident. But if zero rows return, that only tells us nothing was logged in that specific table during that time window. It does not prove the earlier alert was a false alarm, and it doesn't mean an external log process found no errors.
Because this SOP doesn't cover external log extraction, the agent rightly marks the overall result as INCONCLUSIVE. It reports what the database query found, cites the tool step, and leaves the remediation action blank.
If you build your own system, always separate the alert time from the observation time. To prove what caused a past alert, you need evidence from when the alert actually happened. Seeing a healthy system an hour later only proves it recovered—it doesn't explain why it broke in the first place."
Slide 10: RCA as a Foundation for Autonomous Manufacturing
(Target: ~30 seconds)
"Looking ahead, this exact architecture can support broader semiconductor investigations. We could connect equipment telemetry, inspection defects, and lot history to check whether a spike in defects lines up with a specific tool or maintenance event. Over time, once specific recovery actions are proven reliable, they could be qualified for safe automation. Those are future extensions—what we have built today is the traceable investigation and controlled execution foundation to get us there."
Slide 11: RCA Architectures for Different Knowledge Bases
(Target: ~1 minute 30 seconds)
"Finally, if you are looking to build something similar, your architecture should match the kind of troubleshooting knowledge your team actually has.
•	If you have detailed, reviewed SOPs: Our pattern is a direct fit. The SOP strictly defines the allowed checks and how to interpret the results.
•	If you have partial runbooks and past incident tickets: You can lean more on retrieval. Let the model pull relevant past incidents, then check if they actually match the live evidence. Just treat a past ticket as a lead to test—it shouldn't automatically authorize old commands or assume the root cause is identical.
•	If you have sparse SOPs, but strong telemetry: Start with an investigation assistant. Give it system context and approved, read-only diagnostic APIs so it can propose hypotheses and gather data within those bounds, while engineers review unfamiliar steps or uncertain conclusions.
Regardless of which design you choose, carry four guardrails across all of them: check that evidence is fresh, lock down tool permissions, cap the number of agent steps, and verify the final outcome.
